;; bytes-bounds — the constructor witness: a fresh buffer knows its own length.
;;
;; The other three fixtures take a bytes[64] as a PARAMETER and read or write it.
;; This one CONSTRUCTS a buffer. `(bytes-zero)` is admissible only as the whole
;; body of a def with a literal `-> bytes[n]` return (§13.12), and that same
;; annotation is what gives the constructor its length: the emitter reads `32`
;; off the return type and emits the axiom `bytesLen(r) = 32` alongside the
;; constant-zero array itself. Without that axiom the length is not derivable at
;; all — `Map_default(0)` is a total function in the array theory and carries no
;; length — so the post could not be discharged from the body.
;;
;; The length is read from the DECLARED RETURN, never from the post. Writing
;; `(post (= (bytes-length result) 16))` here does not make the program verify;
;; it makes it REFUTED, because the constructor still reports 32.
;;
;; The axiom's validity is a TRUST-channel dependency, not a solver discharge:
;; it holds because codegen reads the same annotation to emit an n-length zero
;; value, so it rides the codegen_semantics_version stamp. Same category as
;; bytes-set's length-preservation fact.
;;
;; Verifies SAFE, including under --strict-verified-core.

(def make-buffer [] -> bytes[32]
  (post (= (bytes-length result) 32))
  (bytes-zero))
